Background. The words in the README, before lesson 0
====================================================

This section exists because the README (and the lessons that follow) assume
you already know what a "pass", "IR", and "CFG" are. It is not part of the
numbered lessons; read it once, then start at lesson 0.

### What is a compiler, and where does LLVM fit?

A compiler translates a program you wrote in a language like C++ into
machine instructions a CPU executes. Modern compilers are built in stages,
and each stage reads one representation of the program, inspects or
improves it, and hands a new representation to the next stage:

```text
C++ source  ->  [front end]  ->  LLVM IR  ->  [mid-end: passes]  ->  LLVM IR'
            ->  [back end]  ->  machine code  ->  object file  ->  executable
```

**Clang** is the front end: it turns `.c`/`.cpp` files into LLVM IR.
**LLVM** is the rest of the pipeline's infrastructure: the intermediate
representation itself, plus libraries of analyses (things that inspect the
IR and report facts about it) and transformations (things that rewrite it),
and the tools to load and run them.

MiniShield does not compile C++ programs. It is a *plugin* loaded into
`opt`, LLVM's command-line IR tool (below), which then runs MiniShield's
passes over `.ll` IR files.

### What is LLVM IR?

LLVM IR (intermediate representation) describes a program in terms of
typed values and instructions, before any specific CPU is chosen. A
minimal example (this is the entire `minishield-input.ll` fixture):

```llvm
define i32 @add_one(i32 %x) {
entry:
  %result = add i32 %x, 1
  ret i32 %result
}
```

- `@add_one` is a **function**; `i32` is a 32-bit integer type; `%x` is a
  typed value.
- `entry:` names a **basic block**: a straight-line run of instructions with
  a single entry point and no jumps inside it. A block always ends with a
  **terminator** instruction that transfers control (`ret`, `br`, ...).
- `%result = add i32 %x, 1` is one **instruction**; the name `%result` is
  defined exactly once and only used after that definition. This
  "define once, use later" property is called **SSA** (static single
  assignment).
- The files in `tests/ir/` (for example `tests/ir/phi.ll`) are handwritten
  text IR, one small function each.

### What is a pass?

A **pass** is one program-level inspection or transformation that LLVM runs
over the IR. A pass receives an object (here: a `Function`), inspects or
changes it, and returns a result describing what it did. Think of a pass as
a function that LLVM calls for you, once per function, in the order you
scheduled.

MiniShield has two so far: `StatsPass` only *counts* instructions and
prints a report (a read-only pass), and `FlatteningPass`, which will
*restructure* the function's control flow (a transformation pass, not yet
implemented).

### What is `opt`, and what is a pass plugin?

`opt` is LLVM's command-line tool for IR: it reads an IR file, runs a
**pipeline** of passes, and writes IR back (or just prints diagnostics).
Out of the box, `opt` only knows LLVM's built-in passes.

A **pass plugin** is a small shared library (here
`build/libMiniShield.so`) that `opt` loads at start-up when you pass
`-load-pass-plugin=...`. The loading happens through the system's
`dlopen()` mechanism: the library is read into memory and its exported
entry point `llvmGetPassPluginInfo` is called. That entry point hands back
the pass names the plugin provides (`minishield-stats`,
`minishield-flatten`), so after loading, `-passes='function(minishield-stats)'`
can name *your* pass. The plugin is therefore a *load-time* relationship
with `opt`, not a link-time one (lesson 1 explains why that decides the
CMake library type).

The comma in `-passes='function(minishield-stats),verify'` is the pipeline:
run the named function pass on every function, then run the built-in
`verify` pass over the module.

### What are control flow, a CFG, and control-flow flattening?

**Control flow** is the order in which a program's instructions execute,
determined by its branches. An `if` statement means "these instructions,
or those, depending on a condition", and a `while` loop means "these
instructions, possibly again".

The **control-flow graph (CFG)** makes that order explicit: basic blocks
are nodes, and every possible "go to next block" transfer is an edge. The
function in `tests/ir/phi.ll` has four blocks:

```text
entry -> left  -> join
       \       /
        right ->   (join ends the function with ret)
```

`entry` has two successors (its conditional branch can choose either),
`join` has none (it ends in `ret`).

**Control-flow flattening** is a transformation that takes a structured CFG
like that and rewires it into a shallower, harder-to-follow shape: many
small basic blocks, all at the same level, joined by a dispatcher — usually
a loop plus a big `switch` over block identifiers that decides which block
runs next. The program still computes the same results, but the visible
control flow becomes one long sequential loop instead of the original
if/else or loop structure. Flattening is used in binary hardening and
obfuscation (attackers' tools find it harder to reconstruct the original
logic), which is why MiniShield's goal is a *safe* flattener: it must
accept only functions whose flow it fully understands and leave everything
else byte-for-byte untouched.

**Natural loops** (mentioned in the README and lesson 16) are the loops a
specific standard algorithm finds: take a **back edge** (an edge from a
block to an earlier block that *dominates* it — dominates meaning every
path from the entry to that block passes through it) and collect every
block that can reach the loop header without going through the exit.
LLVM's `LoopAnalysis` computes exactly these; in `tests/ir/loop.ll` the
edge `body -> header` is the single back edge, and the natural loop is
`{header, body}`.

### What do "function eligibility" and "diagnostic stub" mean?

**Eligibility** is the yes/no decision made *before* a flattening pass
rewrites a function: is this function's shape one the pass can handle? The
current policy (lesson 22) rejects, among other things, functions with
exception handling, coroutine intrinsics, block addresses used as values,
or terminators the pass does not model (only `br`, `switch`, `ret`,
`unreachable` are accepted). An accepted function is **eligible**; a
rejected one must be left completely unchanged — lesson 24 shows how to
prove that with a diff. Eligibility is a conservative "we can handle this
shape" decision, not a proof that flattening is safe in general.

A **stub** is a placeholder implementation: the interface exists and runs,
but the real work is not there yet. At the start of this project,
`FlatteningPass::run` only prints the function name and block count; it
changes nothing. The word **diagnostic** (as in "diagnostic stub") says the
stub's job is to *report observations*, not to transform. By lesson 22 the
stub also prints the eligibility decision.

### What are conda, CMake, and Ninja, in one line each?

- **Conda** (here via **Miniforge**, a conda distribution that defaults to
  the conda-forge channel) is a package and *environment* manager. The
  `minishield` environment is a self-contained directory with its own
  compiler, LLVM 23.1.5, build tools, and libraries, so the project does
  not depend on what the host machine happens to have installed.
  "Activating" it puts that environment's tools at the front of the
  shell's command search path.
- **CMake** is the *configure* step: it reads `CMakeLists.txt`, locates
  the installed LLVM, and *generates* build instructions. It does not
  compile anything itself.
- **Ninja** is the *build* step: it executes the generated instructions
  (`build/build.ninja`), compiling only the changed files and linking the
  plugin.

The four-step chain — create the environment, activate it, configure with
CMake, build with Ninja — is exactly the lesson 0 checkpoint.

### What do the `opt` flags in the README mean?

```bash
opt -load-pass-plugin="$PWD/build/libMiniShield.so" \
  -passes='function(minishield-stats),verify' \
  -S tests/ir/phi.ll -o build/phi-output.ll
```

- `-load-pass-plugin=...` loads MiniShield's `.so` so its pass names exist.
- `-passes=...` is the pipeline to run; `function(X)` means "run X once
  per function in the module".
- `verify` is a built-in pass that checks the IR's structural rules.
- `-S` prints the result as *text* IR; without it, `opt` writes compact
  *binary* IR by default.
- `-o file` writes the transformed IR to a file; `-disable-output` keeps
  the IR in memory and writes nothing — used when you only want the
  diagnostics the pass printed.

### What is a fixture?

A **fixture** is a small, stable input file kept in the repository (here
the `.ll` files under `tests/ir/`) whose expected results are known by
hand. You run your tool on the fixture and compare the output to the
expectation (the table in lesson 11). "Fixture" comes from test setup being
*fixed in place*: nobody edits a fixture to make a test pass.

### Valid vs. correct: what `verify` does and does not prove

`verify` checks that the IR obeys LLVM's *structural* rules: types agree,
every value is defined exactly once before use (SSA), every PHI node has an
incoming value from each predecessor block, return types match the
function's type, and so on. IR that passes is **valid** (well-formed).

**Correctness** — does the transformed program compute the same results,
with the same side effects, as the input — is a separate, stronger
property. `verify` does not check it: a pass can emit perfectly valid IR
that behaves differently. That is why lesson 13's empty-diff check (the
pass must leave the IR unchanged while it is a stub) and, later, real
testing, are the actual safety nets.

0. Create the minishield Conda environment
==========================================

Goal: give the project one consistent Linux C++ toolchain, LLVM release, build system, and test library.

The recorded environment was originally created with this command (confirmed in its conda-meta/history):

  conda create -y -n minishield -c conda-forge clang lld llvmdev gtest cmake ninja

Conda downloaded prebuilt packages and their dependencies. It did not compile LLVM from source. That unpinned command resolved LLVM/Clang 23.1.5 in this study session; running it later can select a different release.

### Create the study environment reproducibly

For a fresh setup on Linux x86-64, first install Conda or Miniforge and initialize its Bash integration if necessary. Then, from this repository:

```bash
conda env create -f environment.yml
conda activate minishield
```

The environment name comes from name: minishield in environment.yml. Creating the environment installs packages into a separate prefix; activating it changes the current shell's PATH and sets CONDA_PREFIX so commands resolve to this toolchain. Activation does not compile this project.

If Conda is installed but activation is unavailable in Bash, load the shell hook from your own installation. In the recorded machine it was:

```bash
source "$HOME/miniforge3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh"
conda activate minishield
```

The setup file pins clang, lld, and llvmdev to 23.1.5 to keep the plugin's headers and runtime compatible. It is a study environment specification, not a byte-for-byte lockfile of every dependency. Package availability and platform still matter.

### Know what each dependency does

| Package | Why the project needs it |
|---|---|
| clang=23.1.5 | C/C++ compiler for the plugin and future input programs |
| llvmdev=23.1.5 | LLVM headers, library development files, and CMake package metadata |
| lld=23.1.5 | LLVM linker available for toolchain work |
| gtest=1.18.0 | GoogleTest for the later C++ test suite |
| cmake | Configures the project and generates Ninja build instructions |
| ninja | Executes the generated compile and link rules |
| libstdcxx-devel_linux-64=16.2.0 | Standard C++ headers such as type_traits, matching this session's runtime |

The initial environment had the C++ runtime but lacked its development headers. During the build lesson we added libstdcxx-devel_linux-64 16.2.0. The repository specification includes it from the start. A missing type_traits error concerns C++ standard-library headers, not the path to LLVM headers.

For an existing environment missing those headers, the normal package-install command is:

```bash
conda install -n minishield -c conda-forge libstdcxx-devel_linux-64=16.2.0
```

### Verify the environment before building

```bash
conda activate minishield
clang --version
clang++-23 --version
opt --version
llvm-config --version
cmake --version
ninja --version
```

The LLVM tools should report the same 23.1.5 release. This environment exposes clang++-23 rather than a plain clang++; pin that compiler in CMake instead of falling back to the host compiler.

Before linking in this environment:

```bash
export LIBRARY_PATH="$CONDA_PREFIX/lib"
```

LIBRARY_PATH supplies link-time library search paths used by the compiler driver. It is different from LLVM_DIR, which tells CMake where to find LLVMConfig.cmake, and from LD_LIBRARY_PATH, which affects runtime library loading.

Checkpoint: distinguish these four operations: install the environment, activate the environment, configure the project with CMake, and build the project with Ninja. They are separate steps.



1. Understand what CMake is configuring
=======================================

Task: read CMakeLists.txt and identify the target named MiniShield.

add_library(MiniShield MODULE ...) describes a loadable plugin. The listed
.cpp files are compiled and linked into build/libMiniShield.so. The MODULE
library is loaded by opt at runtime through -load-pass-plugin.

We separated three CMake questions:

  target_include_directories: where does the compiler search for headers?
  target_link_libraries: which compiled libraries does the target depend on?
  set_target_properties: which named settings apply to the target?

In this project, the include directories contain LLVM's headers and our own
include/ directory. Linking to LLVM uses an imported target supplied by
find_package(LLVM REQUIRED CONFIG). That target describes an existing shared
library; our project does not build LLVM itself.

LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY chooses where the plugin is written. With Ninja
and the current configuration, that is build/libMiniShield.so.

PRIVATE means a requirement applies to MiniShield without advertising it
as a requirement for consumers. PUBLIC applies to both; INTERFACE applies
to consumers. MiniShield's plugin dependencies are private.

Checkpoint: explain why finding a header and linking a library are separate
operations. Including a declaration does not supply compiled implementation.

### Deep dive: Why `find_package(LLVM REQUIRED CONFIG)`?

`find_package(<name>)` can locate a package in two different ways, and the
`CONFIG` keyword selects which:

| | Without `CONFIG` (module mode, default) | With `CONFIG` |
|---|---|---|
| File it hunts for | `FindLLVM.cmake` | `LLVMConfig.cmake` (or `llvm-config.cmake`) |
| Who wrote it | A CMake community contributor, shipped **inside CMake itself** | The **LLVM package author**, shipped *with the package* (e.g. `$CONDA_PREFIX/lib/cmake/llvm/` for the conda-forge `llvmdev` package) |
| How it works | A hand-maintained script that probes paths and compilers | Declares the exact truth about *this build*: imported targets (the `LLVM` dylib target), variables (`LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS`, `LLVM_DEFINITIONS`, `LLVM_PACKAGE_VERSION`), and helpers (`llvm_map_components_to_llvmlib`) |
| For LLVM specifically | Nothing to find — CMake ships ~162 `Find*.cmake` modules and **no** `FindLLVM.cmake` | Works: the config file is installed with the LLVM package |

So `find_package(LLVM REQUIRED CONFIG)` means: *"the package name is `LLVM`;
don't look for a CMake-contributed finder script — find the configuration file
that the installed LLVM package ships."*

**How the search resolves** (config mode), in priority order:

1. `LLVM_DIR` — a variable named after the package + `_DIR`. This build passes
   `-DLLVM_DIR=$CONDA_PREFIX/lib/cmake/llvm`, which is the top-priority hint
   and removes all ambiguity.
2. `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` (if set).
3. System prefixes (`/usr/local`, `/usr`, …), probing subpaths such as
   `<prefix>/lib/cmake/llvm` and `<prefix>/share/cmake/llvm`.

**The three "package" layers** — the same word does three jobs here:

```
conda package   "llvmdev"          → the distribution unit on disk (what was installed)
CMake package   "LLVM"             → the logical contract: "I want to consume LLVM"
config file     "LLVMConfig.cmake" → the file that fulfills the contract (what CONFIG selects)
```

`find_package(LLVM ...)` names the **CMake** package; `CONFIG` selects the
**file** that answers for it; the conda package is just the delivery
mechanism that installed that file.

**Rule of thumb:** if the vendor ships a `FooConfig.cmake`, use
`find_package(Foo CONFIG)`. Find-modules are for ancient libraries that
predate config files. Config files are maintained by the people who *built*
the library, so they're accurate for that exact build (correct C++ standard,
component list, dylib imported targets); community Find-modules are often
years behind, and for LLVM they don't exist at all.

### Deep dive: Do the two search modes affect artifact size or rebuild time?

**No — the search mode is a pure discovery mechanism.** `find_package` is a
*finder*, not a *builder*: it runs once at configure time and only fills in
variables and imported targets (`LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS`, `LLVM_DEFINITIONS`, the
`LLVM` imported target, …). Whichever mode supplies those, the same
downstream decisions are made by the same `add_llvm_library` /
`target_link_libraries` calls:

```
find_package (module mode) ─┐
                            ├─► same variables / imported targets ─► same flags ─► same link line ─► identical .so
find_package (config mode)  ┘
```

If a `FindLLVM.cmake` existed and pointed at the *same* installation that
`LLVMConfig.cmake` points at, the result would be byte-identical: same
include dirs, same definitions, same `libLLVM-23.so` on the link line — no
recompilation, no size change. The mode only becomes visible if the two routes
disagree about *which* LLVM they find; then the difference comes from
different installations, not from the mode.

The three axes, kept separate:

| Axis | What it decides | Affects artifact size? | Forces recompilation? |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Search mode** (module vs config) | *how* CMake locates the package's metadata | No | No |
| **Which installation** is found (e.g. conda 23.1.5 vs anything else) | headers, ABI, `LLVM_DEFINITIONS` | No | Yes — different headers/ABI → our sources recompile |
| **Link strategy** (dylib vs static; see below) | whether LLVM code lives *in* our `.so` | **Yes** — tens of KB vs hundreds of MB | No (link-time only) |

So: **mode = discovery, installation = compatibility, link strategy = size.**
`CONFIG` buys nothing in bytes or build time — it buys *correctness and
determinism* (the package author's own truth about their build, and for LLVM
the only route that exists at all).

### Deep dive: Where does the size go? (`MODULE` + dylib linking)

Measured in this environment (conda-forge LLVM 23.1.5):

- `libLLVM.so.23.1` (the shared library): **188 MB** — prebuilt, already loaded by `opt`
- the static component archives (`libLLVMCore.a`, `libLLVMPasses.a`, …): **363 MB total**

The small-plugin outcome comes from the **`MODULE` + dylib-linking**
combination, not from the `find_package` mode:

- **This project:** `MiniShield` is built as a `MODULE` (a `dlopen()`-able
  `.so` containing *only* our ~5 `.cpp` files — tens of KB). With
  `LLVM_LINK_LLVM_DYLIB ON`, every LLVM symbol resolves **at load time** from
  the *same* `libLLVM-23.so` that `opt` uses — one LLVM in the process.
- **Static alternative** (linking the component archives): those symbols are
  copied into our `.so` → it bloats toward hundreds of MB, *and* you get
  duplicate-LLVM-in-one-process corruption (two sets of globals), *and* every
  relink re-copies that pile. Bigger and wrong, not smaller and safer.
- **`SHARED` vs `MODULE`:** on Linux they produce nearly identical `.so`
  files — the difference is contractual, not mechanical. Details in the next
  section.

Recompilation story: editing MiniShield code → recompile 5 files, relink a
small `.so` (seconds). Switching LLVM releases → recompile our files against
the new headers. We never compile LLVM itself (hours/days) — consuming the
prebuilt 188 MB shared library is the point. You can't hot-swap the LLVM `.so`
under a running `opt`, so versions must match: **one release only.**

### Deep dive: What does `add_library` do?

`add_library(<name> [STATIC | SHARED | MODULE] [EXCLUDE_FROM_ALL] <srcs>…)`
creates a **library target**: a named node in CMake's build graph carrying
sources, compile/link rules, and the *usage requirements* attached by the
`target_*` commands (below). The type keyword sets the artifact and its
contract (Linux):

| Type | Artifact | Contract | Other targets may link it? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `STATIC` | `libX.a` (object archive) | code is *copied into* each consumer at its link time | yes — every consumer embeds its own copy (how you get "two LLVMs in one process") |
| `SHARED` | `libX.so` with a soname | *linked against* at build time; consumers record a `DT_NEEDED` entry | yes |
| `MODULE` | `libX.so` | *loaded at runtime* via `dlopen()` — a plugin, not a dependency | **no** — CMake refuses to link a MODULE into another target |

**Why `MODULE` for MiniShield:** the relationship between `opt` and the
plugin is `dlopen()` at runtime — a load-time, not link-time, relationship.
Nothing ever *links against* MiniShield, so `STATIC` is out (an archive is
not a loadable image) and `SHARED`, while mechanically near-identical on
Linux, would declare a "you may link me" relationship that doesn't exist.
`MODULE` declares the plugin contract — which is also why all link scopes
are `PRIVATE`.

Mechanically, for a real target
(`add_library(MiniShield MODULE lib/Plugin.cpp lib/FlatteningPass.cpp)`):

1. one **compile rule per source** → `build/CMakeFiles/MiniShield.dir/…o`,
   compiled with the target's standard, definitions, and include dirs;
2. one **archive/link rule** → the artifact, by default named
   `libMiniShield.so` (default `lib` prefix) at the build root — pinned
   explicitly by `LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}`;
3. the **target node** itself, which `ninja -C build` builds and the
   `target_*` commands attach properties to.

**The same command has a second role — describing things you do *not*
build.** `LLVMConfig.cmake` line 1775 contains:

```cmake
add_library(LLVM SHARED IMPORTED)
```

An *imported* target builds nothing; it is pure metadata describing an
existing binary (`libLLVM-23.so`: location, soname, link interface).
`target_link_libraries(MiniShield PRIVATE LLVM)` links against that
description, resolved to the real `.so` at link time. One command, two
directions:

```
add_library(MiniShield MODULE …)   producer:  "build this artifact from these sources"
add_library(LLVM SHARED IMPORTED)  consumer:  "here is an existing .so, its properties are…"
```

(`add_llvm_library` — unavailable here, see below — was simply LLVM's
in-tree wrapper around `add_library` with extra LLVM build plumbing; plain
`add_library` is the correct consumer-side replacement.)

### Deep dive: What do `target_include_directories`, `target_link_libraries`, and `set_target_properties` do?

These commands configure the `MiniShield` target created by `add_library`.
Compilation and linking happen later, when the build runs.

**`target_include_directories` — where to find headers.**

```cmake
target_include_directories(MiniShield PRIVATE
    ${LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS}
    ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/include)
```

This adds LLVM's header directories and this project's `include/` directory
to MiniShield's compiler search paths (typically through `-I` options).
It lets source files include LLVM and project headers; it does not link
LLVM's compiled implementation. `PRIVATE` applies these paths to MiniShield
without exposing them as requirements for consumers.

**`target_link_libraries` — which libraries to link.**

```cmake
target_link_libraries(MiniShield PRIVATE LLVM)
```

`LLVM` is an imported target supplied by `find_package(LLVM REQUIRED CONFIG)`.
CMake uses its metadata to locate the existing LLVM shared library and
construct MiniShield's link command. Target dependencies can also contribute
usage requirements, such as include paths and compile definitions.
Here, shared linking records a runtime dependency on LLVM instead of copying
LLVM's implementation into the plugin. `PRIVATE` keeps LLVM out of
MiniShield's public link interface.

**`set_target_properties` — configure named settings on the target.**

```cmake
set_target_properties(MiniShield PROPERTIES
    LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR})
```

The general form is `set_target_properties(<targets> PROPERTIES <name> <value>…)`.
Here, `LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` chooses where to write the built module;
`${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}` is the top-level build directory. Configuring with
`cmake -S . -B build` and building with Ninja therefore produces
`build/libMiniShield.so`. This changes the destination, not the filename or
link dependencies. Multi-configuration generators may append a configuration
subdirectory such as `Debug/`.

### Deep dive: Why target-scoped `target_*` instead of `add_definitions` / `include_directories`?

You'll find the classic LLVM-consumer snippet in older docs and tutorials:

```cmake
separate_arguments(LLVM_DEFINITIONS_LIST NATIVE_COMMAND ${LLVM_DEFINITIONS})
add_definitions(${LLVM_DEFINITIONS_LIST})
include_directories(${LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS})
```

It *works*, but both `add_definitions(...)` and `include_directories(...)` are
**directory-global**: they apply to *every* target defined in that directory
(and subdirectories, until overridden) — your pass, and every test or tool you
add later. The modern style is **target-scoped**, i.e. each target declares
its own requirements:

```cmake
separate_arguments(MS_LLVM_DEFS NATIVE_COMMAND ${LLVM_DEFINITIONS})
target_compile_definitions(MiniShield PRIVATE ${MS_LLVM_DEFS})
target_include_directories(MiniShield PRIVATE
    ${LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS}
    ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/include)
```

Why prefer target scope:

- **No leakage.** Flags and include paths attach to the target that actually
  needs them; future targets (Day-3 test binaries, benchmark harnesses) start
  from a clean slate instead of inheriting LLVM's definitions by accident.
- **Explicit dependency graph.** A target's requirements are readable on the
  target itself, which is exactly what CMake's *usage requirements* model
  (`PRIVATE` / `PUBLIC` / `INTERFACE`) is built around.

The scope keywords, for reference:

| Keyword | Affects this target's own compilation | Affects consumers linking to it |
|---|---|---|
| `PRIVATE` | yes | no |
| `PUBLIC` | yes | yes (and re-exports transitively) |
| `INTERFACE` | no | yes |

`MiniShield` is a `MODULE` (a `dlopen()`-able `.so` that nothing links to), so
everything is `PRIVATE`.

Two more details from that snippet worth keeping:

- `separate_arguments(... NATIVE_COMMAND ...)` does **real work** here:
  `LLVMConfig.cmake` sets `LLVM_DEFINITIONS` as a single *space-joined
  string* (e.g. `"-D_GNU_SOURCE -D__STDC_FORMAT_MACROS ..."`), not a CMake
  list, so it must be split into individual `-D` flags before use. The
  `NATIVE_COMMAND` mode selects the current platform's command-line quoting
  rules; on Linux it uses Unix-style parsing.
- The tutorial's `add_subdirectory(<pass name>)` is a *layout* choice (one
  subdirectory per pass/chapter), not a linking mechanism. This project uses a
  single top-level `CMakeLists.txt` and adds exactly one subdirectory later:
  `tests/`, for the standard CTest/GoogleTest setup.

### Deep dive: What does `separate_arguments` do?

CMake has no array type: a "list" is a string whose elements are separated
by `;`. Two distinct representations therefore exist for "several things":

```cmake
set(X a b c)                    # stored as "a;b;c" — a 3-element list
set(Y "a b c")                  # stored as ONE element containing spaces
```

`LLVMConfig.cmake` defines `LLVM_DEFINITIONS` with the *second* form — one
quoted, space-joined string (line 367):

```
"-D_GNU_SOURCE -D_GLIBCXX_USE_CXX11_ABI=1 -D__STDC_CONSTANT_MACROS -D__STDC_FORMAT_MACROS -D__STDC_LIMIT_MACROS"
```

so it is a **1-element** "list". Passed as-is to
`target_compile_definitions`, CMake would emit it as a *single*
shell-quoted compiler argument — one giant "macro name" containing spaces and
`=` — which the compiler rejects. What the compiler needs is separate
`-D…` arguments.

`separate_arguments(<out> <mode> <in>)` fixes the representation by splitting
on unquoted whitespace:

```
before:  1 element(s) → '-D_GNU_SOURCE -D__STDC_FORMAT_MACROS -D__STDC_LIMIT_MACROS'
after:   3 element(s) → -D_GNU_SOURCE;-D__STDC_FORMAT_MACROS;-D__STDC_LIMIT_MACROS
```

The second argument selects the *quoting rules* used while splitting — it
only matters when the string contains escaped quotes (CMake's internal
command-line representation uses `\"`):

| Mode | Rules applied |
|---|---|
| `NATIVE_COMMAND` (default) | conventions of the machine CMake runs on (Linux → Unix rules) |
| `UNIX_COMMAND` | always Unix rules |
| `WIN32_COMMAND` | always Windows rules (for strings captured from a Windows command line) |

With a quote-free string on Linux, all three behave identically, so
`NATIVE_COMMAND` is just the portable default (and what the canonical LLVM
docs snippet uses).

**When you need it:** only when a variable is a *space-joined string* rather
than a CMake list. Variables you create yourself (`set(X a b c)`) are already
proper lists; `LLVM_DEFINITIONS` is the exception because the package author
wrote it as one quoted string. Rule of thumb: before using a string *as a
list* (foreach, `target_*` call, command arguments), know which of the two
representations you're holding.

### Deep dive: A hello-world CMakeLists to start from

The smallest CMakeLists.txt that does anything useful is three lines:

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.15)
project(HelloWorld CXX)
add_executable(hello main.cpp)
```

- `cmake_minimum_required` sets a version floor: CMake refuses to run if it
  is older than this.
- `project(HelloWorld CXX)` names the project and enables the C++ compiler.
- `add_executable` defines one target: a program. CMake compiles `main.cpp`
  and links `build/hello`.

With that file, `cmake -S . -B build && cmake --build build` works and you
run `./build/hello`. Notice what is *not* there: no package discovery, no
compiler flags, no include paths, no link libraries, no output location.
Every one of those omissions is possible because hello-world has no
dependencies and produces a program that *you* run directly.

MiniShield's CMakeLists.txt has none of that by accident: every extra line
exists because the project consumes a prebuilt third-party library and
produces a plugin that `opt` loads, not a program you run.

### Deep dive: MiniShield's CMakeLists, line by line

The project's file makes four moves.

**Move 1 — pick the language and standard (lines 1–6).**

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.15)
project(MiniShield CXX)
set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)
set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)
set(CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS ON)
```

Same opening as hello-world, plus decisions hello-world can skip: LLVM 23's
headers require C++20, so the standard is pinned (`REQUIRED ON` makes a
downgrade an error, not a warning), and
`CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS` writes `build/compile_commands.json` so
clangd and IDEs can index the project.

**Move 2 — find LLVM (lines 10–12).**

```cmake
set(LLVM_LINK_LLVM_DYLIB ON)
find_package(LLVM REQUIRED CONFIG)
message(STATUS "Found llvm ${LLVM_PACKAGE_VERSION} at ${LLVM_DIR}")
```

Hello-world needs no packages; this project needs the prebuilt LLVM the
Conda environment installed. `find_package(LLVM REQUIRED CONFIG)` locates
the `LLVMConfig.cmake` that the configure step's `-DLLVM_DIR` points at;
that file defines `LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS`, `LLVM_DEFINITIONS`, and the imported
`LLVM` target this file uses in the next moves. `REQUIRED` makes a missing
LLVM a hard configure error instead of a later, mysterious failure.

**Move 3 — define the target (lines 16–22).**

```cmake
add_library(MiniShield MODULE
    lib/Plugin.cpp
    lib/FlatteningPass.cpp
    lib/StatsPass.cpp
    lib/CFGAnalyzer.cpp
    lib/FunctionEligibility.cpp)
```

This is the hello-world divergence in one keyword. Hello-world says
`add_executable`: build a program with a `main()`. MiniShield says
`add_library ... MODULE`: build a loadable shared library with no
`main()`. Nothing links against MiniShield; `opt` `dlopen()`s it at run
time. (`STATIC` would be an archive no one can `dlopen`; `SHARED` would
declare a "you may link me" relationship that does not exist — see the
`add_library` deep dive above.)

**Move 4 — attach the requirements (lines 24–35).**

```cmake
separate_arguments(MS_LLVM_DEFS NATIVE_COMMAND ${LLVM_DEFINITIONS})
target_compile_definitions(MiniShield PRIVATE ${MS_LLVM_DEFS})
target_include_directories(MiniShield PRIVATE
    ${LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS}
    ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/include)
target_link_libraries(MiniShield PRIVATE LLVM)
set_target_properties(MiniShield PROPERTIES LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR})
```

Hello-world compiles against the standard library and needs none of this.
The plugin has four requirements, all `PRIVATE` because nothing consumes
MiniShield: the LLVM compile definitions (split into real `-D` flags
first), the LLVM and project include directories, the link against the
imported `LLVM` target — which is `libLLVM-23.so`, the same library `opt`
already loads, so one LLVM per process — and a pinned output location so
the plugin lands at `build/libMiniShield.so` where the `opt` commands in
the README point.

**Side by side.**

| Question | hello-world | MiniShield |
|---|---|---|
| Artifact | an executable — a program you run | a MODULE shared library — a plugin opt loads |
| Who uses it | you, via `./build/hello` | `opt`, via `-load-pass-plugin` (`dlopen` at run time) |
| `main()`? | required | none — a plugin is entered by opt, not by the OS |
| Dependencies | the standard library only | LLVM 23, discovered by `find_package(LLVM REQUIRED CONFIG)` |
| C++ standard | the compiler's default | C++20, forced — LLVM 23 headers require it |
| Extra flags | none | `LLVM_DEFINITIONS` (split by `separate_arguments`) + LLVM include dirs |
| Linking | implicit | `PRIVATE` against the imported `LLVM` target (`libLLVM-23.so`) |
| Output | `build/hello`, by default | `build/libMiniShield.so`, pinned by `LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` |

Every difference traces back to one sentence: hello-world produces a
program *you* run, with no dependencies, while MiniShield produces a plugin
*opt* loads that must match opt's exact LLVM release. The three-line file
works because it has no decisions to make; the 35-line file is mostly the
record of decisions it does.


