17. Understand references, pointers, and addresses while implementing the CFG
=============================================================================

The learner drafted CFGAnalyzer.cpp and assigned BB directly to info.block.
The review identified a type mismatch: BB is a reference to a block, while
info.block holds a pointer to a block.

Start by reading the declaration in the block loop:

  for (const llvm::BasicBlock &BB : F)

The & in this declaration makes BB a reference. A reference is another name
for an existing object; it is not a pointer variable. Use it like the object:

  BB.getName()

Compare that with the member declaration:

  const llvm::BasicBlock *block;

The * declares a pointer, which stores an object's address. To assign the
address of the block that BB refers to, write:

  info.block = &BB;

Here & is an operator in an expression: it obtains the block's address.
It does not copy the block or create a separate reference object. A compiler
may implement references using addresses internally, but that does not make
a reference interchangeable with a pointer in C++ source code.

Read each form according to its context:

  const llvm::BasicBlock &BB    Declare a reference to a block.
  &BB                          Obtain the referenced block's address.
  const llvm::BasicBlock *ptr   Declare a pointer to a block.
  *ptr                         Access the object that ptr points to.
  BB.getName()                 Access a member through a reference.
  ptr->getName()               Access a member through a pointer.

Now compare the successor loop:

  for (const llvm::BasicBlock *successor : llvm::successors(&BB)) {
      info.successors.push_back(successor);
  }

successor is already a pointer because its declaration uses *. Append it
directly. Taking &successor would obtain the address of the local pointer
variable, not the address of the block. That would add another pointer level
and would not match the vector's element type.

Apply the same reasoning to predecessors. These pointers do not own the
blocks; the function owns them. They must not be used after the referenced
blocks have been deleted or the analysis result has become invalid.

The implementation review also supplied three related corrections:

  Use Result result{}; rather than Result result = new Result();.
  new returns a pointer, while run() returns a Result value.

  Use llvm::BasicBlock with two colons for namespace qualification.

  Use llvm::successors() and llvm::predecessors(), both plural.

Checkpoint: explain why info.block needs &BB, while push_back(successor)
needs no additional &. Then fix the implementation yourself, add its source
to CMake, and build before registering the analysis with LLVM.

### Deep dive: references and addresses

In this loop, `BB` is a **reference** to an existing block, not a pointer:

```cpp
for (const llvm::BasicBlock &BB : F) {
    // BB is another name for the block; access members with BB.getName().
}
```

A reference acts as another name for an object. A pointer holds an object's
address. Since `BlockInfo::block` is declared as
`const llvm::BasicBlock *block`, assign it the referenced block's address:

```cpp
info.block = &BB;
```

`&BB` obtains the address of the existing block; it does not copy the block
or create a separate object representing the reference. The `&` symbol has
different meanings in a declaration and an expression:

| Syntax | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `const llvm::BasicBlock &BB` | Declare a reference to a block |
| `&BB` | Obtain the address of the block that `BB` refers to |
| `const llvm::BasicBlock *ptr` | Declare a pointer to a block |
| `*ptr` | Access the object pointed to by `ptr` |
| `BB.getName()` | Access a member through an object or reference |
| `ptr->getName()` | Access a member through a pointer |

The successor loop already declares its variable as a pointer, so append it
directly, without taking its address again:

```cpp
for (const llvm::BasicBlock *successor : llvm::successors(&BB)) {
    info.successors.push_back(successor);
}
```


18. Read pointer and reference syntax in traversal loops
========================================================

The learner asked why some traversals use * and others use &. Start with
the type of each range's elements, rather than memorizing one loop pattern.

  for (const llvm::BasicBlock &BB : F)

Iterating a function gives access to its basic block objects by reference.
BB is a reference to each existing block. It does not copy the block. Use
BB.getName() to access members. The instruction loop follows the same rule:

  for (const llvm::Instruction &I : BB)

Compare the successor traversal:

  for (const llvm::BasicBlock *successor : llvm::successors(&BB))

The successors range yields block pointers, so successor is a pointer. The
loop copies an address, not a BasicBlock. Use successor->getName(). The &BB
inside the function call obtains the block's address because this overload
of successors() takes a pointer. That expression is distinct from the & in
the declaration of the outer loop's reference variable.

Now examine the analysis result, whose types you defined yourself:

  for (const auto &info : cfg.blocks)

cfg.blocks is a vector of BlockInfo records. const auto &info refers to
each stored record. Without &, auto info would copy the record and its
successor/predecessor vectors on every iteration. Avoid those unnecessary
copies when only inspecting the records.

  for (const auto *successor : info.successors)

info.successors is a vector of pointers to const blocks. Each element is
already a pointer, so copying it is appropriate. The block itself is not
copied. The predecessor loops work the same way.

Likewise, in:

  const auto &cfg = AM.getResult<CFGAnalyzer>(F);

the reference lets StatsPass inspect the cached result without copying its
records and vectors. The analysis manager owns that cached result.

Checkpoint: annotate these four operations in your own words:

  BB.getName()          Access through an object/reference.
  &BB                   Obtain that object's address.
  successor->getName()  Access through a pointer.
  *successor            Access the object identified by the pointer.

The rule is to follow the element type: references for existing records or
objects when avoiding copies, pointer variables when the elements already
hold addresses. There is no rule that every traversal needs * or every
traversal needs &. Keep const when the traversal is read-only.

### Deep dive: Why different traversal loops use `&` or `*`

The loop variable's type follows what the range supplies. A range-based
`for` loop does not require one particular pointer or reference syntax.

```cpp
for (const llvm::BasicBlock &BB : F) {
    for (const llvm::BasicBlock *successor : llvm::successors(&BB)) {
        // BB refers to a block; successor points to a destination block.
    }
}

for (const auto &info : cfg.blocks) {
    for (const auto *successor : info.successors) {
        // info refers to a stored record; successor is a copied pointer.
    }
}
```

Read the loops one at a time:

| Range | What it supplies | Loop variable | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `F` | Basic block objects by reference | `const BasicBlock &BB` | Inspect each existing block without copying it |
| `BB` | Instruction objects by reference | `const Instruction &I` | Inspect each existing instruction without copying it |
| `llvm::successors(&BB)` | Pointers to successor blocks | `const BasicBlock *successor` | Each element already identifies a destination by address |
| `cfg.blocks` | `BlockInfo` records | `const auto &info` | Inspect a stored record without copying its vectors |
| `info.successors` | Stored block pointers | `const auto *successor` | Copy each pointer value, not the pointed-to block |

`llvm::predecessors(&BB)` and `info.predecessors` follow the same pointer
pattern as successors. `const auto &cfg = AM.getResult<CFGAnalyzer>(F)` also
uses a reference to avoid copying the analysis manager's cached result.

In `llvm::successors(&BB)`, `&BB` is an expression that obtains the block's
address because this API accepts a block pointer. It is separate from the
reference declaration `BasicBlock &BB` in the outer loop.

For an ordinary `std::vector<BlockInfo>`, writing `auto info` would copy
each record, including its vectors. Writing `const auto &info` avoids that
copy. For a `std::vector<const BasicBlock *>`, copying an element copies
only a pointer; it does not copy the block. Using `&` everywhere is therefore
unnecessary, and using `*` everywhere would not match the ranges' types.

Pointers and references also determine member access: use `BB.getName()`
and `info.successors`, but `successor->getName()` and
`info.block->getName()`. To pass a pointed-to block to an API that expects a
block reference, dereference the pointer with `*successor`.


19. Understand why auto alone tries to copy a basic block
=========================================================

During the function eligibility exercise, the learner asked why this loop
cannot replace the explicit reference declaration:

  for (auto bb : F)

Teach the difference between type deduction and reference binding. auto
deduces the type, but a plain auto variable does not automatically preserve
the reference returned by the iterator. It tries to create a new local
BasicBlock value by copying the existing element. LLVM BasicBlock is
noncopyable, so that loop does not compile.

The appropriate read-only traversal is:

  for (const auto &bb : F) {
      // Inspect the existing block without copying it.
  }

Here const auto &bb is equivalent to const llvm::BasicBlock &bb. The & asks
for reference binding, while const makes access through that reference
read-only.

Compare the declarations:

  auto bb          Try to copy the element into a local value.
  auto &bb         Bind to the existing element, preserving its constness.
  const auto &bb   Bind to the existing element through a const reference.

There is a useful detail in FunctionEligibility::check: F is declared as
const llvm::Function &. Iterating it gives const block references. Thus
auto &bb also deduces a const reference here; it cannot remove constness
from the block. Writing const auto &bb states the read-only intention
explicitly and remains clear when reading the loop on its own.

Connect this lesson to the earlier pointer traversal. For a vector whose
elements are block pointers, auto successor copies only a pointer value.
It does not copy the pointed-to BasicBlock. Plain auto is therefore not
always wrong: its suitability depends on the element type and whether a
copy is intended.

Checkpoint: explain why auto bb fails for F, why const auto &bb succeeds,
