### Deep dive: Why not `for (auto bb : F)`?

`auto` deduces a type; it does not automatically preserve the reference
provided by the iterator. This loop tries to copy each block into a new
local variable:

```cpp
for (auto bb : F) {
    // Tries to copy a BasicBlock; LLVM BasicBlock is noncopyable.
}
```

Use a reference to bind to the existing block instead:

```cpp
for (const auto &bb : F) {
    // Read the existing block without copying it.
}
```

For this traversal, that is equivalent to declaring the loop variable as
`const llvm::BasicBlock &bb`.

| Loop variable | Effect |
|---|---|
| `auto bb` | Tries to create a value by copying the element |
| `auto &bb` | Refers to the existing element; whether it is mutable follows the range's element type |
| `const auto &bb` | Refers to the existing element through a read-only reference |

`FunctionEligibility::check` takes `const llvm::Function &F`, so the blocks
obtained from that function are already const. Even `auto &bb` therefore
deduces a const reference in this context. Writing `const auto &bb` makes
the read-only intent explicit.

This also explains why `auto successor` can be valid when iterating a vector
of block pointers: it copies a pointer value, not a block. Choose the loop
variable based on the range's element type and whether copying is intended.

Taking `&successor` would obtain the address of the local pointer variable,
not the block's address. The stored block pointers refer to blocks owned by
the function and must only be used while those blocks remain valid.


20. Explain exception-handling pads and personality checks
==========================================================

During function eligibility work, the learner asked why both
F.hasPersonalityFn() and BB.isEHPad() check exception handling, then asked
what the word pad means.

Start with the control-flow picture. Pad comes from landing pad: a
designated place where execution arrives when an exception interrupts
normal control flow. An invoke instruction names two destinations:

  %result = invoke i32 @callee()
      to label %normal
      unwind label %exception

Normal completion continues at normal. An exceptional unwind transfers
control to exception. In the landing-pad model, exception begins with a
landingpad instruction after any PHIs:

  exception:
    %ex = landingpad { ptr, i32 } cleanup
    resume { ptr, i32 } %ex

This is a fragment inside a function with a suitable personality routine,
not a complete standalone IR module. landingpad receives exception
information. cleanup marks participation in cleanup handling; it does not
execute destructors or other cleanup work automatically. Such operations
would be separate instructions. resume continues unwinding.

Teach the broader term next: an EH pad is an exception-handling entry
instruction. LLVM recognizes landingpad, catchpad, cleanuppad, and
catchswitch as EH-pad instructions.

Distinguish the two queries by scope:

  F.hasPersonalityFn()
    Function-level: does this function specify a personality routine?
    That routine defines language-specific exception-handling behavior.

  BB.isEHPad()
    Block-level: is the block's first non-PHI instruction an EH pad?

Use tests/ir/instruction-kinds.ll as the concrete example. with_invoke has
a personality routine, but only its exception block is an EH pad:

  entry      invoke instruction       isEHPad() is false
  normal     return instruction       isEHPad() is false
  exception  landingpad instruction   isEHPad() is true

An invoke initiates a call with an exceptional destination; it is not
itself an EH pad. A pad marks where exception-handling control arrives.

Clarify the overlap in the eligibility checker instead of claiming both
checks are independently necessary. A function may retain a personality
routine without having any pad blocks. Conversely, valid EH-pad
instructions require a personality routine. Rejecting all personality
functions at the start already rejects functions with valid EH pads.
The later block-level rejection is therefore redundant for those inputs,
though it states the unsupported block kind explicitly.

Checkpoint: identify which block receives the unwind edge, explain why
the block containing invoke is not the pad, and explain why early
personality rejection prevents reaching the pad check in this fixture.

Reference: https://llvm.org/docs/ExceptionHandling.html

### Deep dive: Exception-handling pads and personality routines

In exception handling, **pad** comes from **landing pad**: a designated
place where control arrives when an exception interrupts normal execution.
An LLVM `invoke` has separate normal and exceptional destinations:

```llvm
%result = invoke i32 @callee()
    to label %normal
    unwind label %exception
```

If the call succeeds, control continues at `%normal`. If it unwinds with an
exception, control transfers to `%exception`. In the landing-pad model, that
destination begins with a `landingpad` instruction, after any PHIs:

```llvm
exception:
  %ex = landingpad { ptr, i32 } cleanup
  resume { ptr, i32 } %ex
```

This fragment belongs inside a function with a suitable personality routine.
`landingpad` receives exception information, `cleanup` marks participation
in cleanup handling, and `resume` continues unwinding. The marker does not
perform cleanup work by itself; actual cleanup operations would be separate
instructions before `resume`.

An **EH pad** is the broader term for exception-handling entry instructions.
LLVM recognizes `landingpad`, `catchpad`, `cleanuppad`, and `catchswitch` as
EH-pad instructions. See [LLVM's exception-handling documentation](https://llvm.org/docs/ExceptionHandling.html).

The two eligibility checks inspect different levels:

| Query | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `F.hasPersonalityFn()` | Does the function specify a personality routine defining its exception-handling behavior? |
| `BB.isEHPad()` | Is the block's first non-PHI instruction an EH-pad instruction? |

For `with_invoke` in `tests/ir/instruction-kinds.ll`, the function has a
personality routine. Its `entry` block contains `invoke` but is not an EH
pad; `normal` contains a return and is not an EH pad; `exception` starts with
`landingpad` and is an EH pad. Containing `invoke` therefore does not itself
make a block an EH pad.

A function can have a personality routine without any EH-pad blocks. For
valid IR, EH-pad instructions require an associated personality routine.
Consequently, if MiniShield rejects every function with a personality
routine before inspecting blocks, the later `isEHPad()` rejection is
redundant for those valid inputs. It states the block-level restriction
explicitly rather than providing an independent necessary rejection.


21. Relate intrinsics to instructions during coroutine checks
=============================================================

The learner asked how an intrinsic relates to an instruction after adding
coroutine detection to the eligibility checker.

Start with three IR instruction fragments:

  %a = add i32 %x, 1
  %b = call i32 @my_function(i32 %a)
  %c = call double @llvm.sqrt.f64(double %value)

All three lines are instructions. The first is an add operation. The second
calls an ordinary function. The third calls an intrinsic: a special function
with semantics defined by LLVM itself.

An intrinsic is commonly expressed as a function call rather than a new
instruction opcode. Its name has the reserved llvm. prefix. For example,
declare the square-root intrinsic without supplying a function body:

  declare double @llvm.sqrt.f64(double)

LLVM knows the intrinsic's behavior and lowers it to appropriate target
code. It need not remain a machine-level function call. Distinguish the
function declaration from the call instruction: the declaration is a
Function, while the call inside a basic block is an Instruction.

Next connect the concepts to the C++ class hierarchy:

  Instruction
    CallBase
      CallInst
        IntrinsicInst

Each line is derived from the class above it. Every IntrinsicInst is both
a CallInst and an Instruction. Not every Instruction is a call, and not
every call invokes an intrinsic. LLVM permits certain intrinsic uses through
invoke as well; IntrinsicInst specifically represents intrinsic calls with
the call opcode.

Now explain the eligibility check:

  llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::IntrinsicInst>(&I)

This checks whether I is an intrinsic call instruction. Success yields a
pointer that exposes intrinsic-specific information; failure yields nullptr.
For a successful cast, inspect getCalledFunction()->getName(). A name
starting with llvm.coro. identifies the coroutine family.

Use both checks in the initial coroutine policy: reject a function marked
isPresplitCoroutine(), and inspect instructions for coroutine intrinsic
calls. The latter policy is conservative: it also rejects ordinary functions
that manipulate coroutine handles through these intrinsics. Only accept a
function after all function, block, terminator, and instruction checks pass.

Connect this back to the statistics exercise. An intrinsic call still has
opcode Call, so the existing Call case counts it. The counter currently
means all call instructions, including intrinsic calls, rather than only
calls to user-defined functions.

Checkpoint: classify add, an ordinary function call, and an intrinsic call.
Explain why the coroutine name check is needed after dyn_cast succeeds:
recognizing an intrinsic alone does not establish that it is a coroutine
operation; llvm.sqrt.f64 is an intrinsic too.

Reference: https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#intrinsic-functions

### Deep dive: Intrinsics and instructions

An **instruction** is one operation in LLVM IR. An **intrinsic** is a special
function whose meaning LLVM already knows, commonly invoked through a `call`
instruction. Compare these instruction fragments:

```llvm
%a = add i32 %x, 1
%b = call i32 @my_function(i32 %a)
%c = call double @llvm.sqrt.f64(double %value)
```

| Result | Instruction opcode | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `%a` | `add` | Built-in arithmetic operation |
| `%b` | `call` | Call to an ordinary function |
| `%c` | `call` | Call to an LLVM intrinsic |

`llvm.sqrt.f64` has LLVM-defined semantics. Declare it without providing a
function body:

```llvm
declare double @llvm.sqrt.f64(double)
```

Intrinsic names use the reserved `llvm.` prefix. LLVM lowers intrinsic
operations to appropriate target code; an intrinsic call does not necessarily
remain a function call in machine code. See [LLVM's intrinsic documentation](https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#intrinsic-functions).

In the C++ API, the relevant inheritance relationship is:

```text
Instruction
  └── CallBase
       └── CallInst
            └── IntrinsicInst
```

Every `IntrinsicInst` is an instruction and a call, but most instructions
and many calls are not `IntrinsicInst`s. The intrinsic function declaration
itself is a `Function`; the instruction inside the caller is the operation
that invokes it. LLVM also permits certain intrinsics in `invoke`
instructions; `IntrinsicInst` specifically represents intrinsic `call`
instructions.

For coroutine checking, this expression asks whether `I` is an intrinsic
call instruction:

```cpp
llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::IntrinsicInst>(&I)
```

On success, inspect `getCalledFunction()->getName()` to identify the family.
The `llvm.coro.` prefix identifies coroutine intrinsics, which the initial
eligibility policy rejects conservatively. A failed cast returns `nullptr`.

The statistics pass's `Call` opcode counter also counts intrinsic calls:
they still have opcode `Call`. Being recognized as an intrinsic does not
change the instruction's opcode into a separate intrinsic opcode.


