Glossary. Terms used in the README and Day 1
============================================

Alphabetical, one-to-three-sentence definitions of the terms that appear in
the README or in this guide, for readers who met them before they were
explained. "See lesson N" points to where the term is used; the lesson's
deep dives go further than the entry here. Entries marked (Background) are
covered by the unnumbered Background section at the start of this file.

**ABI (application binary interface).** The machine-level contract between
compiled code and its libraries: calling convention, object layout, and
which shared-library symbols exist. Switching the LLVM release changes the
headers and ABI, which is why the plugin must be built against exactly the
LLVM release that `opt` runs (see lesson 1, "which installation" row).

**Address-taken block.** A basic block whose *address* is used as a value
in the IR (produced by `blockaddress`). Such a block is a value a program
can compute with, not just a control-flow destination, so the flattening
policy rejects it (see lesson 23).

**`add_library`.** A CMake command that creates a library *target* — a
named node in the build graph carrying sources, compile/link rules, and
requirements. The type keyword (`STATIC` / `SHARED` / `MODULE`) decides the
artifact (see lesson 1, "What does add_library do?").

**`awk`.** A small text-processing language for line-by-line field work.
`awk -F ', '` splits each input line into fields at the first
comma-plus-space separator (see lesson 12).

**Back edge.** An edge `A -> B` in a CFG where `B` **dominates** `A` (every
path from the function entry to `A` passes through `B`). Every natural loop
has at least one; in `loop.ll` the edge `body -> header` is the only back
edge (see lesson 16).

**Basic block.** A straight-line run of instructions with one entry point,
no jumps inside, and a terminator at the end. Basic blocks are the nodes of
a CFG (Background).

**`blockaddress`.** An LLVM operation that yields the address of a named
basic block as a value: `ret ptr blockaddress(@f, %target)`. It is what
makes a block "address-taken" (see lesson 23).

**Binary IR.** LLVM IR serialized to a compact binary format (`.bc` files);
`opt`'s default output. `-S` requests text IR instead (see lesson 6).

**`build.ninja`.** The file CMake + the Ninja generator write into
`build/`: the complete list of compile and link rules. "Ninja could not
find build.ninja" means configuration did not complete (see lesson 2).

**CMake list.** CMake has no array type; a "list" is a string whose
elements are separated by `;`. A space-joined string is *one* element,
which is why `LLVM_DEFINITIONS` must pass through `separate_arguments`
before use (see lesson 1, "What does separate_arguments do?").

**`clang` / `clang++`.** The C and C++ compilers of the LLVM project. The
conda environment exposes `clang++-23` (no plain `clang++`), so the
configure command pins that path explicitly (see lessons 0, 2).

**Conda / Miniforge.** A package and environment manager (Miniforge is a
common conda distribution that defaults to the conda-forge channel). The
`minishield` environment is a self-contained directory with one consistent
toolchain — its own compiler, LLVM 23.1.5, and build tools — so the project
does not depend on the host machine's C++ setup (see lesson 0).

**Conda environment.** A named directory prefix holding one consistent set
of packages. Creating it installs packages into that prefix; activating it
only changes the current shell so the environment's tools are found first
(see lesson 0, checkpoint).

**Conda prefix.** The directory an environment is installed into (for
example `~/miniforge3/envs/minishield`). "Separate prefix" means packages
do not mix with the system's or other environments' (see lesson 0).

**`CONDA_PREFIX`.** An environment variable set by `conda activate`,
holding the path to the active environment. Recipes like
`$CONDA_PREFIX/bin/clang++-23` and `export LIBRARY_PATH=$CONDA_PREFIX/lib`
are how you point CMake and the linker at that environment's tools (see
lessons 0, 2).

**Coroutine.** A function that can be suspended and resumed. In LLVM IR it
is expressed through `llvm.coro.*` intrinsics. A **presplit coroutine** is
a coroutine function the front end has already split into continuation
blocks, detectable with `F.isPresplitCoroutine()` (see lessons 21, 22, 24).

**C++ standard library headers (for example `<type_traits>`).** The header
files of `libstdc++`, the C++ runtime. Conda installs the *runtime*
(`libstdc++.so`) and its *development headers* as separate packages; a
missing `<type_traits>` means the `libstdcxx-devel_linux-64` package is
absent, not that an LLVM header is misconfigured (see lesson 0).

**Declaration vs. definition (function).** A *declaration*
(`declare i32 @foo(i32)`) announces that a function exists, with no body;
a *definition* (`define i32 @foo(i32 %x) { ... }`) provides the body.
Passes only run on definitions; `F.isDeclaration()` detects the former (see
lessons 8, 21).

**`dlopen()`.** The POSIX function that loads a shared library at run time
and returns a handle to its symbols. This is the mechanism behind
`-load-pass-plugin=...`: the plugin is a load-time relationship with `opt`,
not a link-time one (see lesson 1).

**Dominator.** Block B *dominates* block A when every path from the
function entry to A passes through B. Dominance underpins the back-edge
definition and the `DominatorTreeAnalysis` (see lesson 16).

**dylib.** A shared library. LLVM's dylib in this environment is
`libLLVM-23.so` (about 188 MB); `opt` loads it, and the plugin resolves its
LLVM symbols against the same copy at load time (see lesson 1, "Where does
the size go?").

**EH pad.** The first instruction of a block that receives exceptional
control: `landingpad`, `catchpad`, `cleanuppad`, or `catchswitch`. The name
comes from *landing pad*. `BB.isEHPad()` asks whether the block's first
non-PHI instruction is one of these (see lesson 20).

**`fallthrough`.** In a C `switch`, control continuing into the next `case`
because the current case has no `break`. A missing `break` after a branch
case was the counting bug of lesson 10.

**Fixture.** A small stable input file with known-by-hand expected results
(here, the `.ll` files in `tests/ir/`); "fixed in place" as test setup
(Background).

**`friend`.** A C++ declaration granting another type access to private
members. `friend llvm::AnalysisInfoMixin<CFGAnalyzer>;` lets the mixin
reach the analysis's private `AnalysisKey` (see lesson 15).

**GoogleTest (gtest).** A C++ unit-testing framework; installed in the
environment now and used by the later C++ test suite (see lesson 0).

**`Instruction.def`.** A file in LLVM's source that lists every opcode
name; the authoritative spelling reference for this version (`CondBr`,
`UncondBr`, `Switch`, ...). There is no `Instruction::Br` in LLVM 23 (see
lesson 10).

**`isa<T>(X)` / `dyn_cast<T>(X)`.** LLVM's type-testing and type-casting
helpers: `isa` answers "is this value a T?" and returns a boolean;
`dyn_cast` returns a `T*` on success and `nullptr` otherwise (see lessons
10, 21).

**Intrinsic.** A special function declared by LLVM itself, with names in
the reserved `llvm.` namespace (for example `llvm.sqrt.f64`,
`llvm.coro.noop`), whose semantics LLVM knows and which is lowered directly
to target code. In IR it appears as an ordinary `call` instruction with
opcode `Call` (see lesson 21).

**IR / LLVM IR.** The typed, SSA intermediate representation that compiler
stages operate on; `.ll` files are text IR (Background).

**`LIBRARY_PATH`.** An environment variable the compiler driver reads for
*link-time* library search directories. Conda's clang does not search
`$CONDA_PREFIX/lib` by default, so the export makes `libstdc++.so`
findable. It is a different thing from `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` (which affects
*runtime* library loading) and from `LLVM_DIR` (which tells CMake where the
LLVM CMake package lives) (see lessons 0, 2).

**`lld`.** The LLVM linker; installed in the environment for future
toolchain work (see lesson 0).

**Landing pad.** The block a thrown exception "lands" on; see EH pad
(lesson 20).

**`LoopAnalysis` / natural loop.** LLVM's standard loop finder. A natural
loop is a back edge's target (the header) plus every block that can reach
the header without going through the exit. `getLoopsInPreorder()` lists
nested loops in tree **preorder** (parent before children); iterating the
`LoopInfo` object directly visits only the outermost loops (see lesson 16).

**`minishield-input.ll`.** The smallest fixture: one function, one block,
one `add`, one `ret` (see lesson 6).

**`MODULE` / `SHARED` / `STATIC`.** The three CMake library types:
`STATIC` builds an object archive (`libX.a`) whose code is copied into each
consumer; `SHARED` builds a linkable shared object (`libX.so`) consumers
record a `DT_NEEDED` entry for; `MODULE` builds a loadable plugin
(`libX.so`) that nothing links against and that is loaded via `dlopen()`
(see lesson 1).

**Mixin.** A base class that contributes one concern — here, pass metadata
such as the pass name — to a pass class. `OptionalPassInfoMixin` vs.
`RequiredPassInfoMixin` decide whether the pass may be skipped (see
lesson 4).

**`ninja` / "no work to do".** The build tool that executes
`build/build.ninja`. "No work to do" means every rule's outputs are up to
date; it is not a test result and not a failure (see lessons 1, 2).

**`opt`.** LLVM's IR command-line tool: read IR, run a pipeline of passes,
write IR and diagnostics (Background).

**`optnone`.** A function attribute meaning "do not optimize this
function". Optional passes (like `FlatteningPass`) are skipped on
`optnone` functions; required passes (like `StatsPass`) still run there (see
lessons 4, 22).

**Opcode.** The operation code of an instruction: `add`, `icmp`, `br`,
`call`, `ret`, ... `Instruction::getOpcode()` returns it; the exact
spellings in LLVM 23 are listed in lesson 10.

**`opt -S` / `-o` / `-disable-output`.** Print text IR / write the output
to a file / keep the result in memory and print no IR (Background).

**`PATH`.** The colon-separated list of directories the shell searches for
commands. `conda activate` puts the active environment's `bin/` at the
front of it, which is what "activation" accomplishes (see lesson 0).

**Pass.** One program-level inspection or transformation that LLVM runs
over the IR, once per object, in scheduled order (Background).

**PassBuilder.** The C++ class that turns a pipeline string like
`function(minishield-stats),verify` into actual pass instances and managers.
A plugin registers its passes with the `PassBuilder` through the callback
it returns from `llvmGetPassPluginInfo` (see lesson 5).

**Pass pipeline.** The `-passes=` string: an ordered list of pass
invocations with nesting groups such as `function(X)` (run X once per
function), `module(Y)`, or `loop(Z)`; a comma separates consecutive stages
(Background).

**PassPluginLibraryInfo.** The C++ structure a plugin's entry point
returns: the plugin API version, the plugin name, the plugin version, and
the callbacks that register passes with the `PassBuilder` (see lesson 5).

**Personality routine.** The function a language runtime calls while an
exception unwinds, to decide how the exception is handled (in C++ it is
`_Unwind_Resume`). A function that *can* receive exceptions carries one;
`F.hasPersonalityFn()` asks whether it does (see lesson 20).

**PHI node / `phi`.** An instruction that, at the entry of a block, picks
which value flows in from which predecessor:
`%x = phi i32 [ 1, %left ], [ 2, %right ]`. It exists because of SSA:
instead of redefining a value's name at a merge point, the merge point
explicitly selects among incoming values (lesson 6; Background).

**Plugin API version (`LLVM_PLUGIN_API_VERSION`).** A constant from
`llvm/Plugins/PassPlugin.h` identifying which plugin ABI the plugin was
written against; the host checks it matches before loading. It is separate
from the plugin's own version string (see lesson 5).

**Predecessor / successor.** For block B: predecessors are the blocks with
an edge *into* B; successors are the blocks with an edge *out of* B.
`llvm::predecessors(&BB)` / `llvm::successors(&BB)` yield them (see
lesson 14).

**Preorder.** A tree traversal order in which a parent is listed before its
children. `getLoopsInPreorder()` therefore lists an outer loop before the
loops nested inside it (see lesson 16).

**PreservedAnalyses.** The value a transformation pass returns, describing
which cached analyses remain valid after its changes. `::all()` says
"nothing was touched" (correct for read-only passes like `StatsPass`);
`::none()` says "assume every cached analysis is invalid" (the safe default
for a real transformation). It reports cache validity only — it does not
run the verifier or prove behavior was preserved (see lessons 3, 13).

**`#pragma once`.** A compiler directive meaning "include this header at
most once per translation unit" (see lesson 7).

**Release build.** `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`: the compiler is asked to
optimize the plugin and omits debug-oriented checks (see lesson 2).

**Reference vs. pointer.** A *reference* (`const BasicBlock &BB`) is
another name for an existing object; a *pointer* (`const BasicBlock *p`)
stores an object's address. `&BB` takes the address of the referenced
object; `*p` dereferences a pointer (see lessons 17, 18).

**Shared library (`.so`).** A compiled object loaded at run time and shared
by every user of it. `libLLVM-23.so` and `build/libMiniShield.so` are both
shared objects; the first is *linked against* (recorded in `DT_NEEDED`),
the second is *loaded* via `dlopen()` by `opt` (see lesson 1).

**`soname`.** The canonical name recorded inside a shared library, which
consumers record in their `DT_NEEDED` entries so the runtime loader knows
which file to load (see lesson 1).

**SSA (static single assignment).** The rule that every value in LLVM IR
is defined exactly once and used only after that definition. Jumps and
merges are handled by PHI nodes instead of redefining names; `verify`
checks the SSA constraints (lessons 6, 8; Background).

**Static count vs. dynamic count.** A *static* count counts what is
*written* in the IR: one `call` instruction counts as 1 no matter how many
times it executes at runtime. A *dynamic* count would count runtime
executions. Every count in MiniShield so far is static (see lesson 10).

**`stderr` / `stdout` / `2>&1`.** Stream 1 (stdout) carries normal output;
stream 2 (stderr) carries diagnostics — `llvm::errs()` writes there.
`2>&1` merges stderr into stdout so a `$(...)` command substitution can
capture both (see lesson 12).

**Stub.** A placeholder implementation that exists and runs but does not
do the real work yet; a "diagnostic stub" is one whose only job is to
print observations (Background).

**Target (CMake).** A named node in CMake's build graph carrying its
sources, compile/link rules, and the requirements attached by the
`target_*` commands; `add_library` creates one (see lesson 1).

**Text IR.** Human-readable LLVM IR (`.ll`); `-S` requests it. The compact
default is binary IR (see lesson 6).

**Terminator.** The last instruction of a basic block; it transfers
control: `br`, `switch`, `indirectbr`, `ret`, `invoke`, or `unreachable`.
The current flattening policy accepts `CondBr`, `UncondBr`, `Switch`, `Ret`,
and `Unreachable` only (see lessons 10, 22).

**Translation unit.** One `.cpp` file, expanded with the headers it
includes and compiled into one object file. Each translation unit needs the
*declarations* of the classes it uses; the CMake source list decides which
translation units get compiled and linked into the plugin (see lesson 3).

**Unreachable.** A terminator marking a point that cannot execute (for
example after an infinite loop or a checked precondition); it transfers
control nowhere. The flattening policy accepts it (see lesson 22).

**Unwind / `invoke`.** An `invoke` instruction calls with two exits:
`to label %normal` and `unwind label %exception`. *Unwinding* is the
exceptional exit; it lands on an EH pad (see lesson 20).

**Usage requirements (`PRIVATE` / `PUBLIC` / `INTERFACE`).** CMake's model
of *who* sees a target's compile/link requirements: `PRIVATE` = the target
itself only, `PUBLIC` = the target and its consumers, `INTERFACE` =
consumers only. MiniShield is a plugin nothing links to, so everything is
`PRIVATE` (see lesson 1).

**Valid vs. correct.** `verify` proves *validity* (the IR obeys LLVM's
structural rules); it does not prove *correctness* (the transformed
program behaves the same as the input) (Background).

**`verify`.** A built-in pass that checks IR structural validity: types,
SSA constraints, PHI incoming edges, return types, and related invariants.
It succeeds silently on valid IR (Background; lesson 6).
